This week's Q&A Livestream covered two major topics - first the updated rewards for Tombs of Amascut, then a look at the updates to Group Ironman! The JMods covered questions on:
* [[#Question #1|The changes to the ToA rewards]]
* [[#Question #3|Will the Heka of Tumeken ever make it to Old School?]]
* [[#Question #9|How the Group Boss Bash raffle will work]]
* [[#Question #17|Regular ironmen joining unranked GIM groups]]
* [[#Question #31|POH updates for Group Ironmen]]

Thanks to Mod Light (Community Manager), Mod Elena (Content Developer), Mod Husky (Content Developer), Mod Kieren (Associate Design Director) and Mod Arcane (Senior Content Developer) for their insight!

Missed the livestream? Check out the full transcript below!

__TOC__
==Question #1==
{{Cquote2|Arcane, do you want to talk about some of the new changes we proposed in the blog that just went live?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tfyfk9a4GJ0&t=147s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Arcane:''' We’ve already had a big poll and blog with all of the Tombs of Amascut rewards, all of which passed. There are a couple of rewards that we felt could be improved slightly, whether it was we just didn't really like what these rewards have become, or mathematically some weapons weren't really achieving what we would have liked. Some players also just had some really good ideas for alternative rewards! So we wanted to offer some changes.  We released a blog today, and it's got three big things on it. I'll go from smallest to biggest, and most of the questions that I've curated are about the big-ticket item.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' The first one is changing how Osmumten's Fang calculates accuracy. Instead of it rolling for accuracy once, if it rolls and fails, it'll roll again. You get this really interesting damage curve (that we have on the blog) that basically makes it an incredibly accurate weapon! We've also rebalanced some of the stats slightly. We brought it down from 175 accuracy to 105, because with this double accuracy roll it's really, really accurate. We just had to rebalance the stats a bit, but we feel like it's now doing what it was trying to do a lot better without feeling artificial, since 175 accuracy on a one-handed weapon felt a little weird! That's one potential change that if everyone likes, we will go ahead with. It’s effectively the same weapon, it's just doing it slightly differently.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' The second reward was a new reward offered that players suggested which was an upgrade to the rune pouch. The rune pouch can hold three types of runes right now with a capacity of 16k of each type. This upgrade would let you upgrade your pouch into a four-slot rune pouch, you can have 16k of four different types of runes inside of a four-slot rune pouch. This is really, really powerful. There is so much utility you get from magic all over the game. I imagine this is going to be a really, really desirable item. The one caveat is we're thinking about making this item untreatable. The idea is you would have to do ToA to get this reward. That being said, we wouldn’t have it as a crazy rare item that needs hundreds of hours of gameplay, we'd expect it to be fairly common. Maybe you go there and do 20-50 KC and you'd expect to get this item. ToA scales with difficulty. It's not just going to only be this crazy difficult raid, you can customise the difficulty to your nature. We're hoping it is quite approachable for most players to go do ToA, we don't expect you to be an expert at PvM to get it!
* '''Mod Arcane:''' Let us know what your feedback is on this! We think it's a really good idea to have untradable rewards for most content, so every piece of content has purpose for everyone. Right now, Ironmen have to do everything but regular account can just skip 90% of the game and just go sit at TOB until the end of days and make a lot of money, which isn't really great gameplay!
* '''Mod Arcane:''' The last one, which is going to be the bulk of this quickfire question session, is the new mage weapon. The Heka was the previously offered mage weapon - it was a fast-attacking, variable speed weapon with unique spellbook effects. People really liked the idea of this wacky weapon with unique specific interactions and the alternating gameplay, but just didn't really feel as though it would be "enough". We buffed the numbers and we changed a bunch of things around and it objectively was a strong upgrade. It still just didn't feel like that Tbow, it didn't feel like that Scythe. ToA was missing that one item that purely drives the demand of players wanting to do this content for years and years to come just like the Tbow and Scythe always will have.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' So we wanted to revisit that and we didn't think it was right to keep iterating on Heka again and again and again. It gets really confusing and it muddies the water a lot. We're proposing a new mage weapon that would basically replace the Heka. This isn't to say the Heka will never happen ever again. We can use in the future, but for ToA the idea is we would offer this new mage weapon instead of the Heka if it passes.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' This new mage weapon it's called the Shadow of Tumeken. The Shadow of Tumeken is going to be a two-handed 5 tick staff. It's going to have moderate stats - 35 accuracy, 20 defence, nothing special, and a bit of prayer. But does have one really really important effect. While it's equipped, all of your magic strength from gear is tripled and all of your magic accuracy from gear is tripled.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' This makes all of your individual gear switches incredibly valuable. This is going to give Seers ring a lot of accuracy, everything gets more and more powerful. It heavily incentivizes you to bring more and more switches, which differentiates it. We don't have any two-handed 5-tick mage weapons which should make it a bit more interesting to use, and also helps it fit that whole Scythe and Tbow feel.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' We're hoping that it's going to be quite an interest in that regard. That bonus accuracy that you get is actually huge! This is going to make it the most accurate weapon in the game by quite a large margin. It's going to make it actually possible to mage some bosses like Zilyana and Kree'arra, any boss that has pretty high regular defence and pretty high magic defence. The accuracy of this weapon will actually make it viable to use mage those bosses like Ziylana and Kree'arra. If you want more information about what bosses this might actually be good at, check the blog -it has a bunch of examples in there. That's the general idea, I think this is a pretty crazy weapon!

