==Question #8==
{{Cquote2|Lots of MMOs and other popular games use events to boost engagement. One great thing about Old School is how conservatively this is applied. What can you do to reassure players that OSRS won't lean too far into events and other limited releases?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tfyfk9a4GJ0&t=874s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Light:''' As I said earlier, there are no in-game items to be won. This kind of raffle idea is something that we have talked about in the past on live streams and it was received quite positively as a concept. We felt that given the fact that we would like to bring in some changes to Group Ironman in the form of increasing accessibility, we thought this would the perfect opportunity to tempt people who are maybe no longer playing just to come back, or existing players who might want to have a new goal to work towards. We also think it's just something fun, so we're trialling it to see how it goes. We definitely care about the community response. That's why we have a survey for feedback, and we’ll be checking it to see how you guys respond to it.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' I think the first point about sort of how conservative Old School is with events is absolutely so important. That's the thing that I think is at the top of most lists, it's one of these things... as much as we've not really written it down as a golden rule, it's one of these things that is absolutely paramount when when we're thinking about stuff like this. The way I think of it is no matter when you play, whether you want to play tomorrow, tonight or the weekend - it doesn't give you any inherent advantages to your progression on your account, be it skill levelling or access to different rewards. That would rule out something like a double XP weekend, and other events like that. These would obviously increase engagement, but in a way that Old School sees as not a way that fits our philosophy.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Whilst something like a competition, we're seeing a little bit differently. Yes, its goal is still to increase engagement, only this way it's a bit of fun.  A competition is just that, it's separate. It's separate in the way that the rewards are external to your account, and thus, you don't care about membership, and that's not really what you're going for. It's only a nice little extra if you win anyway. It's just a little bit of a nudge, a little bit of fun to have, as opposed to "I need to do this because I'm going to get double experience" or something like that.
* '''Mod Husky:''' I saw a lot of people say "why is it just Group Ironman?". I think it just ties into what you said there. We're trying to lift some of the restrictions and get people back into playing with a friend, maybe come back and do a fun event. A lot of the thought when designing and developing the underlying code for this has been to see how can we make this more of a structure so that if we want apply this to other game modes, like ironmen, mains, the rest of the community - maybe it's not bosses, it's other things are easy to expand on. We're definitely writing it in that sort of way, where it's not gonna be a lot of work for another developer to do that. This all comes down to whether or not players want to have these kinds of events where they're competing for membership, not for a limited item or a boost in their XP or anything like that.
* '''Mod Elena:''' I think it's just a good time to have that competition for Group Ironman now specifically, because we're going to be focusing on group bosses. If you were there at the beginning of Group Ironman when it launched, you're probably at a place now where you're looking to get into bossing. Some people have been bossing for a while already, some are really fast! But I think people who don't play as much are getting to that point now. This could be a really good push to go try something new, perhaps. Your gear might not be optimal but you can still have fun going DKs with your group, and maybe you'll win some membership at the same time!

==Question #9==
{{Cquote2|How will the raffle tickets affect your chance of winning? Because it works as a raffle? Right? It's not like the most amount of people, the most amount of points wins. So can you explain a bit more about that side of things?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tfyfk9a4GJ0&t=1173s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Husky:''' The term "raffle" is just the best way to describe it. I'm not sure if people are familiar with that... If you can imagine, every time you kill a boss a ticket drops into the hat with your name on it. If you've got more tickets in this hat, when we pull out the winners, there is going to be a higher chance your group's name is going to be on this ticket. The first name we pull out would be a lifetime of membership, then the next nine would be a year membership. You could be an all-maxed account, super-efficient, playing 12 hours a day, and you might still only have 5% of the tickets which would be a 1/20 chance, and we all know how drop rates work after playing OSRS so even then you're still not guaranteed but you're definitely increasing your odds. I think this makes it more fair and open to a lot of people. It's less about "how can I optimise and get the most kills per hour", but more just "hey, let's participate in this", and it could change up your activities. For example, say you were going to grind out some primordial boots but you notice the raffle is on and slayer bosses are not included in it. Now maybe instead you'll say to your group "Hey, let's do some nightmare tonight, see if we get lucky and get an Inquisitor's piece of an Orb and we'll also get some raffle tickets on the side!"

==Question #10==
{{Cquote2|Are we planning or looking at implementing the raffle system for other types of accounts?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tfyfk9a4GJ0&t=1280s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Light:''' I can actually speak to that - I looked at the survey results today. I know it's only been 24 hours, but it does seem like the majority of respondents to that survey seemed in favour of implementing this system across other types of accounts, not just group Ironmen.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Absolutely. It's got the potential to do that. Right now it's just an experiment to begin with, and with group Ironmen we like the social dynamics of groups. Getting group Ironmen to do something together is part of the desire here! Now that we've got a system, it can naturally be extended in theory to all accounts, or indeed, different types of content. It doesn't have to be bosses, it could be anything arbitrary. As long as we tell you what it is, and give you a list of points for completion, we could absolutely do that. I think that even ties into one of the ideas we talked about in the Game Jam yesterday, the Seasonal HiScore system. We can provide a competition such as "most agility laps completed". Okay, that maybe isn't the most exciting idea but you can see how this system works. Every lap you complete, you get a ticket. We can even have that on the HiScores so you can see how many tickets you've got compared to other people. I think there's a lot of potential to do more of this. Right now though, we are going to be looking at how well this one works, if the community likes it. Then we base our decision on that. So far there certainly seems like there's the appetite for this to extend to other things too!

